\begin_layout Abstract
In this thesis, a novel technique to create interpretable reinforcement
 learning policies is introduced.
 From an interpretability perspective, it is advantageous if solutions resemble
 known and familiar strategies.
 Therefore, we propose to use genetic programming to elaborate essential
 algorithmic improvements to a domain expert's strategy instead of starting
 from scratch.
 To achieve this, the edit distance to the origin program is used as a complexit
y metric.
 Additionally, the option to select a fixed core in the expert program is
 implemented, which is not affected in the evolutionary process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
The approach is tested on the mountain car and industrial benchmark by performin
g a symbolic regression on transition samples that were collected by a black
 box agent.
 The evaluation shows that the familiarity of expert programs can be preserved
 in GP candidates, making them easier to interpret and having no major drawbacks
 compared to regular runs.
 In cases, where the TED does not protect the desired structure, selecting
 a fixed core may be required.
 The central nodes of an expert program often structure the approach, which
 is the opposite of what regular GP would aim for, leading to results that
 tend to be less overfitted.
 Familiar GP is a new, viable way to create interpretable policies, can
 make GP solutions more robust, unifies the Pareto front and can be used
 without risk as an alternative to regular GP.
\end_layout

