\end_inset

 (RL), enable the autonomous learning of policies in a wide field of technical
 systems.
 For some years now, they have even often outperformed human strategies,
 which offers enormous opportunities for science and industry.
 
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
xxx For domain experts 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
xx In contrast to machine teaching, where techniques are investigated how
 one can facilitate the learning of models, here the machine is supposed
 to lecture the human by showing feasable improvement directly in the its
 students algorithm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
xx Especially in security-relevant areas, where individual wrong decisions
 can have serious consequences, it is therefore difficult to trust them.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 However, machine learning (ML) approaches use technologies whose decision-makin
g process is not explained and regarded as a black box.
 According to Hein 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "daniel_hein_doktorarbeit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Especially for industrial systems, domain experts are more likely to deploy
 autonomously learned controllers if they are understandable and convenient
 to assess
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 This goal is addressed in the field of 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
emph{explainable artificial intelligence} (XAI)
\end_layout

\end_inset

, which has become more and more popular in recent years 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "AIINDEX2019"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, especially since the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

right to explanation
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 was included as a directive in the GDPR regulations 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "EUdataregulations2018"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Explanatory methods include using perturbation (e.g.
 meaningful perturbations
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI-perturbation"
literal "true"

\end_inset

), learning a model locally around the prediction (e.g.
 LIME 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI-LIME"
literal "true"

\end_inset

), function analysis (e.g.
 Taylor Expansion) or model structure analysis (e.g.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
emph{Layerwise Relevance Propagation}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI_LRP_Frauenhofer"
literal "true"

\end_inset

).
 While these techniques help extracting features that contribute to a specific
 decision, they leave large parts of the decision process untouched.
 However, in order to gain trust in an approach, it must be possible to
 understand the whole strategy.
 According to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI-unified-approach"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The best explanation of a simple model is the model itself; it perfectly
 represents itself and is easy to understand.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 The most common method for creating such models is to use evolutionary
 algorithms such as genetic programming (GP), which optimizes a population
 of candidate solutions directly in a feasible region that is constrained
 to be interpretable.
 However, the random nature of ML creates unstructured formulae which can
 feel counterintuitive for experts, making them difficult to understand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We therefore propose to exploit a domain expert's preliminary work of understand
ing a problem, finding an abstracted solution and developing an intelligent
 heuristic accordingly as interpretable anchor point for further explanations
 (see 
