\begin_layout Section
Interpretable Machine Learning
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Interpretable-Machine-Learning"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interpretability is defined as the ability to explain or to present in understan
dable terms to humans 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI_survey"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 In contrast to performance, interpretability has many manifestations and
 is not easy to quantify.
 In 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI_doshivelez2017rigorous"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, Doshi-Velez and Kim propose a subdivision of interpretability into various
 relevant factors, including the following considerations: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
How is the problem formulation incomplete?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
At what level is the evaluation being performed? (application, general user
 study, proxy)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What are task-related relevant factors? (e.g.
 global vs.
 local, severity of incompleteness, level of user expertise, time constraints)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What are method-related relevant factors being explored? (e.g.
 form of cognitive chunks, number of cognitive chunks, compositionality,
 monotonicity, uncertainty)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
RL problems are incomplete as they are not fully testable and can be solved
 in various ways.
 The level of evaluation is not limited, as applications can also be learned
 through proxy models.
 Considering the task-related relevant factors, we are particularly interested
 in global interpretability, as post-hoc explanations for a only a subset
 of decisions is not sufficient to understand and trust policies that depend
 on a sequential MDP.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
More than around the task, the method-related interpretability is the main
 concern for this work.
 Since the effort required to understand something depends on many factors,
 it is difficult to quantify success in concrete terms.
 An abstract breakdown as basic unit of explanation is defined as cognitive
 chunk 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI_doshivelez2017rigorous"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 The most widespread paradigm that is used as indicator for interpretability
 is the number of cognitive chunks, which can be defined as a metric for
 most approaches.
 For example, complexity measures for formulae consider the absolute number
 of mathematical components, and sometimes their complexity (form of cognitive
 chunks).
 However, this metric must ignore other important factors such as the compositio
nality, which is why simple formulas are often still difficult to comprehend.
 
\end_layout

