	lyxscale 50
	width 89text%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The concept of familiar GP, evolving a known expert solution to approximate
 transition samples of a black box.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "img:plausibility-concept"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Familiarity 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Familiarity"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
xx
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Familiarity is introduced as the key paradigm of interpretability, which
 denotes the similarity to a previously known humanly developed strategy.
 As mentioned in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Interpretable-Machine-Learning"

\end_inset

, using simplicity as single deciding factor for interpretability often
 leads to formulae that seem like arbitrary compositions.
 However, defining a metric for interpretability including factors other
 than the number of nodes, let alone combining all relevant factors in a
 single and practical interpretability metric, is sheer impossible, as the
 way in which an expert considers a policy interpretable strongly depends
 on personal experience working on the problem.
 However, an expert program implicitly includes a lot of the desired features
 inherently.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A program is the algorithmic representation of an experts best effort to
 solve a problem.
 It reflects the user experience in its full nature and exploits all preliminary
 work, making it the optimal anchor point for interpretability.
 Familiar chunks of a strategy do not have to be re-understood and allow
 to focus on novelties.
 Furthermore, the compositionality of chunks can be addressed by structuring
 the solution approach in a useful manner.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Tree Edit Distance 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Familiarity-Measurement-TED"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We designate policies as familiar, when algorithmic structures can be recognized
 within an algorithm so that it is easier to understand.
 In order to generate solution candidates that are as familiar as possible,
 we need to define a distance function on a suitable unit of measurement
 which represents the scope of the change, or to put it another way, the
 necessary edits that must be applied.
\end_layout

