\begin_layout Paragraph
Olympus reproduction (Pareto reproduction)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similar to the Greek gods who came to earth to reproduce, the Pareto candidates
 were also given the opportunity to intermingle with the current population.
 In order not to lose already found solutions permanently, the genes of
 the best candidates are to provide a very small current to the population.
 The Olympus reproduction includes the possibility for the origin tree to
 reproduce itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Fixed Tree Core 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Fixed-nodes"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A fixed core comprises a manually selected node structure in the origin
 tree that must be present in all individuals of a population.
 It extends the implicit protection of the TED with a more precise specification
 of familiar structures.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This technique was implemented in response to 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "run:MTC200_MSE_simple"

\end_inset

, where the TED proved to be insufficient protection.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Implementation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The implementation of a fixed core in the reference tree does not only require
 adjustments in the tree structure, but also modifications in the evolutionary
 process.
 A fixed core consists of a real subset of connected nodes, which have to
 be selected manually and root-down.
 At least one mutable junction must be provided for the evolutionary process,
 an example can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "img:tree_modifiable_nodes_modified"

\end_inset

.
 The first layer of mutable nodes can spread over many depth levels, hence
 the tree depth is re-implemented as the distance to the next fixed node.
 Having more than one mutable junction can lead to over-complex and unbalanced
 trees.
 Therefore, construction methods for random trees require a constraint on
 the total number of nodes rather than on the depth.
 
\end_layout

