\begin_layout Subsection
Preset Parabolic Bounding
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This close-form preset policy was presented by Xiao 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "xiao2019"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 and is a structured If-distinction which excludes the option of doing nothing
 and is characterized by a parabolic upper and lower bound (
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
ref{alg:mc-xiao}
\end_layout

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:xiao-origin-plot"

\end_inset

).
 This policy is already fairly optimized, its average reward with -102.6
 is almost as good as the SARSA-200 agent.
 However, the upper bound actually has no function and even mistakenly tries
 to reverse before the goal.
 The computational tree features 26 nodes, making it very robust against
 fundamental changes.
 With this origin, it is particularly interesting to see whether already
 optimized solutions can still benefit from familiar improvements or if
 the results become worse.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All Pareto candidates demonstrated increased real performance, most of them
 even outperforming the SARSA-200 policy (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:xiao-origin-plot"

\end_inset

).
 Selecting a fixed core did exclude using option 1, even though the results
 had a similar online and offline performance.
 Two candidates can be regarded in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:xiao-c5"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:xiao-c8"

\end_inset

.
 Changing the second If-case in the candidates removes the reversal near
 the target, although not completely, which can be explained by a tendency
 in the SARSA-200 policy to choose Action 0 in this area.
\end_layout

