\begin_layout Subsection
Optimized If-Expert
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This man-made heuristic implements early reversal points using if-statements
 (see 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
ref{alg:mc-ifex}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "mc:mc-ifex-plot"

\end_inset

).
 Through an extensive improvement process, two reversal points in If-cases
 have already been handled, otherwise the velocity is maximized.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

With an average reward of $-100.3$, this approach represents the best performing
 expert strategy.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The strategy is also intended to offer the evolutionary process many isolated
 optimization opportunities.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:mc-ifex-pareto"

\end_inset

, all candidates are within a very good performing range, the fixed core
 version performing slightly better.
 For most candidates, many different branches were changed so that complex
 extensions were split between several if-conditions, making even trees
 with high TED easy to interpret.
 One of the best candidates can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:mc-ifex-c8"

\end_inset

.
 Due to the high complexity of the expert tree as well as its comparatively
 high breadth, evolution was most time consuming among all expert strategies.
 A fixed core could greatly improve runtime by inherently preventing many
 highly divergent solutions and allowing for more meaningful crossover and
 mutation of candidates.
\end_layout

