\begin_layout Standard
Minor difficulties can occur in runs with simple strategies, in which a
 few edits already lead to a disintegration of the core structure due to
 the small number of nodes.
 A solution is achieved by manually defining a fixed core, which also had
 a positive effect in complex origin trees by significantly reducing the
 runtime.
 The constraints through fixed nodes, as well as other limitations (e.g.,
 a reduced set of operators, exclusion of actions), did not show a significant
 impact on the regression error.
 On the contrary, it actually had a positive impact by creating a more homogeneo
us population that allowed a better comparison between the individual Pareto
 candidates, who also had a comparatively good and secure online performance.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The TED is well suited as a surrogate measure of familiarity, yet some anomalies
 may occur.
 The complexity and interpretability of a formula especially depicts the
 operators of a tree, while the terminal nodes have a comparatively insignifican
t role.
 Changes to constants in particular fall heavily as full-fledged edits,
 although their optimization process is actually desired.
 This can prevent deliberate simplifications and even lead to artificial
 constructions of operators around the constants in order to protect their
 values.
 Observations are less affected by this problem, but have yet another comparativ
ely important role as their unique set represents the dimension of the input
 vector.
 Taking into account the disproportionate effect of terminal nodes, we propose
 to trim the TED; a simple solution is to exclude constant nodes when computing
 the TED.
 Furthermore, the symmetric distance between the sets of observations can
 be considered to fully exclude terminal nodes from the edit distance.
 Apart from that, it is also recommended to unify and order computational
 trees before calculating the TED, as was proposed in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sec:sympy-tree-reduction"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A general limitation in symbolic regression is the ability to sufficiently
 approximate the hidden strategy of a black box with a function.
 For example, the PSONN policy (IB) is a very fuzzy strategy, so the regression
 error was insufficiently reduced in both the regular and familiar runs.
 This fits with the results of Hein, who described this Problem in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "daniel_hein_doktorarbeit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 To a lesser extent, this problem also occurred with the SARSA-75 policy,
 for which the regression error was more difficult to reduce than with the
 more accurate SARSA-200 version.
 The potential problem of failing to reduce the regression error can be
 indicated by considering the regression error of the experts program.
 If it is too large, either the black box agent is using a sparse strategy,
 or the approaches are fundamentally incompatible.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Overall, the positive effects of familiarity were manifold.
 It provides a practical paradigm to reuse the preliminary abstracted knowledge
 of an expert for better interpretability, it can lead GP to overfit less
 by driving an abstracted approach into a population and has shown no major
 drawbacks or risks compared to regular GP.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Conclusion & Future Work 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "chap:Conclusion"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Conclusion 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:summary"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this work, familiarity, the similarity to an already known solution,
 was introduced as a paradigm for the interpretability of ML policies.
 Reinforcement learning agents are often based on black box technologies
 which need to be explained in order to be trusted.
 We argued, that human-designed programs represent a beneficial anchor point
 for interpretability and trust.
 Based on this, a novel was presented, which uses genetic programming to
 find algorithmic improvements to a domain expert program.
 This was achieved by using the edit distance to the original tree as a
 complexity metric, as well as an optional explicit protection of tree structure
s by selecting fixed nodes.
 The evolutionary process was given the task to find efficient extensions
 so that the functionality of an inexplicably better black box solution
 is achieved, for which a symbolic regression is performed on previously
 collected transition samples.
 To this end, a GP framework was developed that implements these techniques
 as well as some resulting evolutionary principles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The approach was evaluated on the mountain car and the industrial benchmark.
 The evaluation showed that the familiarity of the strategies with the proposed
 techniques can be maintained in the respective candidates without major
 performance decrease.
 The TED allowed various extension to happen, with only a few exceptions
 where the core structure required a manual protection by defining fixed
 nodes.
 Compared to regular GP, familiar candidates were easier to interpret, not
 only because of the similarity of approach, but also because the Pareto
 front became more homogeneous, facilitating a direct comparison of candidates.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This technique can help domain experts to explain how their algorithm can
 be improved to function like a black box or to endorse a certain programmatic
 structure in a GP process to intelligently drive the evolution.
 The limitations mainly concern the ability to sufficiently approximate
 the data, but as failure can be derived from the regression error, familiar
 GP provides a lot potential to profit from.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Future Work 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Future-Work"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section, two extension options are discussed in more detail.
 The first one concerns the TED (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "TED-improvements"

\end_inset

), the second one proposes an explorative regression (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "subsec:Explorative-Regression"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Differentiating and Weighting the Familiarity of Edits 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "TED-improvements"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By using a distance measure such as the edit distance, the question emerges
 whether (and how) the nature of a change can be taken into account.
 Weighting nodes with different complexities is a straightforward approach
 in regular GP, a sample weighting of the commercial GP Framework 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
emph{Eureqa}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:EUREKA-GP-complexities"

\end_inset

.
 To apply a similar weighting to an edit distance, the weighted mapping
 must be computed, since a subsequent weighting of the regular mapping is
 not necessarily the minimum distance.
 A simple answer to the question of whether and how edits should be rated
 is to ignore the altered nodes.
 Another idea is to divide operators into classes, with weighted changes,
